Technical notes first, then general stuff:

   It would be nice if you trimmed out the "junk" parts of the patch ---
the lines where only whitespace changed, and the 380-some lines of file 
/include/pm.h, which is autogenerated. This would make it more obvious
(1) how "big" your patch really is, and (2) what it does.

   Your messages should be proper English, with capitalization and grammar 
and all that jazz. :)  "you see a swearing sword on the ground" is not
"You see a swearing sword on the ground."

   Related: What /is/ a "swearing sword"? It's totally unclear to me 
whether this is a sword such as a knight might swear on, or something
like a "singing sword" except more vulgar. ;)  Is it a reference to some
book or movie?

   Also related: Note that the ground is not always "the ground." Sometimes
the player is standing on ice, or hovering over water, or --- so I hear 
tell --- floating in an air bubble. Grep the source for "^surface(".

   In the "exper.c" change, it would be more efficient to do the uwep test 
first thing, rather than waste all that time computing what tmp "should" 
be and then setting it back to zero.


   So, completely aside from the technical trivia... what's the point of 
this patch? It seems totally unbalancing --- a powerful sword that keeps 
the player from going up in experience level? Excellent! --- and doesn't 
really make sense. What is the "swearing sword," why does kicking a mirror 
give it to you, and what does it have against water nymphs? (And why 
doesn't it have the same effects w.r.t. mountain nymphs?)
   One important angle: Some players /want/ to keep their experience level 
as low as possible, for several reasons. So an artifact that (1) is easy 
for a low-level character to get, (2) kills things quickly, and (3) grants 
no experience, has no downside for them.

   The other major effect of the sword is altar creation. (And printing
a debugging message that probably shouldn't be there.) That's even more
obviously munchkin and imbalancing.


   On the other hand, if this is just practice at making a Nethack patch,
I think you did a pretty good job. Just needs more polishing on the user 
interface --- spelling, grammar, and clever and accurate messages.

